<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@type": "FAQPage",
  "mainEntity": [
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much does walk-in cooler door repair cost?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Our commercial diagnostic is $120, applied toward the repair once you approve the work. Most walk-in door repairs run between $200 and $650. A door gasket, sweep, or latch is toward the lower end; a full closer or cam-lift hinge set, a perimeter heater-wire replacement on a freezer door, or a threshold rebuild runs higher. A full door-leaf replacement is quoted separately. We give a written estimate before any work begins."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "My walk-in door won't stay closed / won't self-close. What's wrong?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A walk-in door that drifts open or slams is almost always the door closer or the hinges. Walk-in doors use spring or hydraulic closers (Kason 1094 SureClose gives a fast initial pull then a slow final close) that lose tension or leak over time. Separately, the cam-lift (cam-rise) hinges are supposed to lift the door slightly as it opens and let it drop to compress the gasket as it closes; when the cams wear, the door sags, stops self-closing, and starts chewing up the gasket. Our techs check the closer and the hinge cams together, because a worn hinge will destroy a brand-new gasket in weeks."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "How much does a walk-in cooler door gasket replacement cost?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Door gasket replacement is one of our most common and most cost-effective walk-in door repairs. It typically runs $200 to $380 installed, depending on door size and gasket type (magnetic perimeter gasket plus, on many doors, a wiper sweep along the bottom). A failing gasket is easy to spot: you feel cold air leaking, see condensation or frost around the door edge, or a dollar bill pulls out easily when closed in the door. Replacing it early cuts the refrigeration load and stops frost problems before they start. We check the hinges at the same visit, since a sagging door is the most common reason a new gasket fails early."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "My walk-in freezer door is frozen shut. Why, and can you fix it?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "A freezer door bonded shut is a freezer-specific failure: the perimeter heater wire around the door frame has failed. Walk-in freezer doors run a self-regulating heater wire (110 to 120V) around the frame perimeter, down the hinge-side track, and across the threshold to keep ice from forming at the gasket contact area. When it fails, condensation freezes and glues the door shut. Our techs free the door with controlled heat to the gasket area (never force, which bends the door), then replace the perimeter heater wire and check the threshold and frame heater. Walk-in coolers running above freezing do not have this failure. Same Day Appliance Repair, $120 commercial diagnostic, waived with repair."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "The inside safety release on our walk-in is broken. Is that urgent?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Yes, treat it as urgent. Every walk-in latch (Kason 0058 Safeguard and equivalents) is designed to accept an inside safety release (such as the 481-C) so a person shut inside can push the door open even when it is latched or padlocked. A broken or missing inside release is an entrapment hazard and a code/OSHA problem, not a convenience issue. We replace the inside release and the latch and strike, verify the door opens from inside under load, and confirm the glow handle is functional. This is one of the few walk-in repairs we advise doing the same day it is found."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Do you replace the whole door, or just the hardware?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "Usually just the hardware, and that is almost always the right call financially. Gaskets, sweeps, hinges (Kason 1245 cam-rise and equivalents), closers (Kason 1094/1095), latches and strikes, inside safety releases, heater wire, thresholds, and vapor-proof door lights are all field-replaceable on a door that is otherwise sound. We only recommend a full door-leaf or frame replacement when the panel core is water-logged, the leaf is bent, or the frame is corroded through, and we give you the honest math first. When a full door or frame swap is the right call, we coordinate it through our commercial partner network."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
      "name": "Which walk-in door brands and hardware do you service?",
      "acceptedAnswer": {
        "@type": "Answer",
        "text": "All of them. Door boxes from US Cooler, Nor-Lake, Master-Bilt, Kolpak, Bally, American Panel, and most others, plus the door hardware they are built with, which is dominated by Kason (closers 1092/1094/1095/1097, cam-rise hinges 1245, Safeguard latches 0058 with 481-C inside release) and Component Hardware. We carry common gaskets, hinges, closers, latches, and freezer heater wire on the van, and source door-specific parts same-day through our commercial distributors."
      }
    },
    {
      "@type": "Question",
